--TEST--
A delegated call whose `by $field` slot was never written throws where the call is written — `rule:classes/delegation-by-field` bullet 2, `rule:classes/an-unwritten-property-read-throws`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:classes/delegation-by-field` bullet 2: "calling a delegated method before `$field` is
// written throws". The forward reads the field and dispatches on whatever
// class the value's own header names, so an unwritten slot is not a
// null-receiver bug one call down — it is `rule:classes/an-unwritten-property-read-throws`'s storage state
// reaching a read, and the forward guards it exactly as a written
// `$outer->inner` is guarded. E0720 admits only a non-nullable class- or
// interface-typed field, and `rule:classes/definite-property-initialization` discharges every one of those at
// its constructor, so `lateinit` (`rule:classes/lateinit`) is the one declaration that can
// reach here at all.
interface Greeter {
  public function greet(): string;
  public function shout(string $suffix): string;
}

class Polite implements Greeter {
  public function greet(): string { return "hello"; }
  public function shout(string $suffix): string { return "HELLO" . $suffix; }
}

class Outer implements Greeter by $inner {
  public lateinit Polite $inner;
  public function attach(Polite $p): void { $this->inner = $p; }
}

int $throws = 0;

Outer $o = new Outer();

// Through the class's own type…
try {
  echo $o->greet(), "\n";
} catch (Throwable $e) {
  $throws = $throws + 1;
  echo "unwritten: ", $e->message, "\n";
}

// …and through the interface, which is the spelling a rewrite at the call
// site would have missed: the forward is a real method, so both dispatch to
// it and both reach the same guard.
Greeter $g = $o;
try {
  echo $g->greet(), "\n";
} catch (Throwable $e) {
  $throws = $throws + 1;
}

// A forwarded member with arguments takes the same edge, and the argument is
// released there rather than handed to a callee that never runs.
try {
  echo $o->shout("!" . "?"), "\n";
} catch (Throwable $e) {
  $throws = $throws + 1;
}

// Writing the field first works exactly like any other write, and every
// later forward sees it — `rule:classes/an-unwritten-property-read-throws`'s third bullet, through the forward.
$o->attach(new Polite());
echo $o->greet(), "\n";
echo $g->greet(), "\n";
echo $o->shout("!"), "\n";

// The direct read of the same field agrees with the forward's own, which is
// the thing neither line can assert alone.
echo "direct=", $o->inner->greet(), "\n";
echo "throws=", $throws as string, "\n";
--EXPECT--
unwritten: `Outer`'s property `$inner` is read before it is written
hello
hello
HELLO!
direct=hello
throws=3
